IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coroners-dates`

-- Coroners Act 1997 (Australian Capital Territory) -- from the seat of a witness: a person summoned or asked to give
-- evidence, produce documents or give information to a coroner (a treating doctor or nurse, an employer or co-worker, a
-- family member), including a person whom the coroner's findings may criticise, and a person who holds the deceased's
-- records (the person in charge of a hospital, aged care facility or other institution).
--
-- "Must I produce what the subpoena asks for? Can it be used against me? Must I answer a question that may incriminate
-- me? Will I get a certificate? The coroner may criticise me: what notice must I get, and by when can I respond? Can I
-- claim my expenses? Am I protected if I tell the coroner something in confidence? How long must I keep the records?"
--
-- The Act's provisions are addressed to coroners; they are encoded as what they mean for the witness. Every opinion,
-- belief or satisfaction left to the coroner is asked as an input. The family member's seat is coroners-family.l4; the
-- owner's or occupier's seat is coroners-owner.l4. Names here are this module's own.
--
-- Edition: Republication No 51 (R51), effective 5 September 2026, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1997-57.txt. The Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 4 (witness expenses, s 98) and
-- the Legislation Act 2001, s 171 (client legal privilege) and s 133 (penalty units) are not deposited.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as
-- advice. See NOTES.md.
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.

§ `Coroners Act 1997 (ACT) -- a witness, a person the coroner may criticise, a holder of records`

§§ `ss 43, 44, 45 -- a subpoena (only if I have been served with one)`

-- s 43(1): the coroner may issue a subpoena requiring a person to appear "(c) to give that evidence or produce that
-- document or thing; or (d) to give that evidence and produce a document or thing ...; or (e) to produce a document or
-- thing ...". (2): "A person is taken to have complied with a subpoena under subsection (1) (a) if the person delivers
-- the document or thing to the court before the date specified in the subpoena." (3): "A person cannot rely on the common
-- law privileges against self-incrimination and exposure to the imposition of a civil penalty to refuse to produce a
-- document or other thing required under a subpoena." Note: "The Legislation Act, s 171 deals with client legal
-- privilege." s 44(3): "A person is not entitled to refuse to comply with a subpoena because of a failure at the time the
-- subpoena was served to give the person the form." s 45(1): if a served person fails to attend, "the coroner may issue
-- a warrant for the arrest of the person."
-- READING (finding 7): s 43(2)'s "subpoena under subsection (1) (a)" is read as a subpoena only to produce a document or
-- thing (s 43(1)(e)); paragraph (a) is a ground, not a kind of subpoena, and delivery cannot satisfy a subpoena to give
-- evidence.
DECLARE `Subpoena answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I must attend and produce it; the privileges against self-incrimination and exposure to a civil penalty are no excuse (s 43(3)), but what is obtained because I produced it cannot be used against me except for an offence about its falsity or an administration of justice offence (s 43(4)); not being given the expenses form is no excuse (s 44(3)); if I do not attend the coroner may issue a warrant for my arrest (s 45)`
    `I have complied: the subpoena only asks me to produce, and delivering the document or thing to the court before the date in it counts as compliance (s 43(2))`
    `not covered: client legal privilege is dealt with by the Legislation Act 2001, s 171 (the note to s 43), which is not encoded`
    `no: nothing under s 43 obliges me until a subpoena has been served on me (s 44); I may still give information voluntarily (s 99B)`

@export I have been served with a coroner's subpoena to produce documents or things. Must I produce them?
GIVEN `a subpoena has been served on me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner's subpoena has been served on you (personally, by signed-for post, or left with a responsible adult at your home or work)
      `the subpoena only asks me to produce` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subpoena only requires you to produce documents or things (not also to give evidence)
      `I delivered them to the court before the date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you delivered the documents or things to the court before the date stated in the subpoena
      `I claim client legal privilege over them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim client legal privilege (lawyer-client privilege) over what is asked for
GIVETH A `Subpoena answer`
DECIDE `Must I produce what the subpoena asks for question` IS
    IF (NOT `a subpoena has been served on me`)
    THEN `no: nothing under s 43 obliges me until a subpoena has been served on me (s 44); I may still give information voluntarily (s 99B)`
    ELSE IF `I claim client legal privilege over them`
         THEN `not covered: client legal privilege is dealt with by the Legislation Act 2001, s 171 (the note to s 43), which is not encoded`
         ELSE IF `the subpoena only asks me to produce` AND `I delivered them to the court before the date`
              THEN `I have complied: the subpoena only asks me to produce, and delivering the document or thing to the court before the date in it counts as compliance (s 43(2))`
              ELSE `yes: I must attend and produce it; the privileges against self-incrimination and exposure to a civil penalty are no excuse (s 43(3)), but what is obtained because I produced it cannot be used against me except for an offence about its falsity or an administration of justice offence (s 43(4)); not being given the expenses form is no excuse (s 44(3)); if I do not attend the coroner may issue a warrant for my arrest (s 45)`

-- s 43(4): "However, any information, document or other thing obtained, directly or indirectly, because of the production
-- of the document or other thing, is not admissible in evidence against the person in a civil or criminal proceeding,
-- other than a proceeding for-- (a) an offence in relation to the falsity or the misleading nature of the document or
-- thing; or (b) an offence against the Criminal Code, chapter 7 (Administration of justice offences)."
DECLARE `Later proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a civil proceeding (for example a claim for damages)`
    `a criminal proceeding for some other offence`
    `a prosecution for an offence about the falsity or misleading nature of the document or thing`
    `a prosecution for a Criminal Code chapter 7 (administration of justice) offence`

DECLARE `Produced documents answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: what is obtained, directly or indirectly, because I produced it is not admissible against me in that proceeding (s 43(4))`
    `yes: s 43(4) does not protect me in a proceeding for an offence about the falsity or misleading nature of what I produced, or for an administration of justice offence (s 43(4)(a), (b))`

@export Can what I produced under a coroner's subpoena be used against me in a later case?
GIVEN `the later proceeding` IS A `Later proceeding` @desc What kind of later proceeding against you. One of: a civil proceeding; a criminal proceeding for some other offence; a prosecution about the falsity or misleading nature of what you produced; a prosecution for a Criminal Code chapter 7 (administration of justice) offence
GIVETH A `Produced documents answer`
DECIDE `Can what I produced be used against me question` IS
    IF     `the later proceeding` EQUALS `a civil proceeding (for example a claim for damages)`
        OR `the later proceeding` EQUALS `a criminal proceeding for some other offence`
    THEN `no: what is obtained, directly or indirectly, because I produced it is not admissible against me in that proceeding (s 43(4))`
    ELSE `yes: s 43(4) does not protect me in a proceeding for an offence about the falsity or misleading nature of what I produced, or for an administration of justice offence (s 43(4)(a), (b))`

-- s 45(1): "If-- (a) a subpoena under section 43 has been served on the person to whom it is directed; and (b) the person
-- fails to attend at the time and place specified in the subpoena; the coroner may issue a warrant for the arrest of the
-- person." (4): the coroner may keep the person in custody or release them on a recognisance; (5): if they then fail to
-- attend, a further warrant and forfeiture of the recognisance.
DECLARE `Arrest answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner may issue a warrant for my arrest, and may then keep me in custody or release me on a recognisance to attend (s 45(1), (4))`
    `no: s 45 allows a warrant only where a subpoena was served on me and I failed to attend at the time and place in it`

@export Can I be arrested for not attending under a coroner's subpoena?
GIVEN `a subpoena has been served on me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner's subpoena has been served on you
      `I failed to attend at the time and place in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you failed to attend at the time and place stated in the subpoena
GIVETH AN `Arrest answer`
DECIDE `Can I be arrested for not attending question` IS
    IF `a subpoena has been served on me` AND `I failed to attend at the time and place in it`
    THEN `yes: the coroner may issue a warrant for my arrest, and may then keep me in custody or release me on a recognisance to attend (s 45(1), (4))`
    ELSE `no: s 45 allows a warrant only where a subpoena was served on me and I failed to attend at the time and place in it`

§§ `ss 48, 51B -- a question that may incriminate me (only if I am giving evidence)`

-- s 48(2): "A coroner may-- (a) require a witness to answer a question put to the witness ..."
-- s 51B(1): the section applies if a witness objects to giving evidence "on the ground that the evidence may tend to prove
-- that the witness-- (a) has committed an offence against or arising under an Australian law or a law of a foreign
-- country; or (b) is liable to a civil penalty." (2): the coroner decides whether there are reasonable grounds. (3): if so,
-- the coroner "must not require the witness to give the evidence" and must tell the witness they need not give it unless
-- required under (4), and that a certificate will be given if they give it willingly or when required. (4): "The coroner
-- may require the witness to give the evidence if the coroner is satisfied that-- (a) the evidence does not tend to prove
-- that the witness has committed an offence against or arising under, or is liable to a civil penalty under, a law of a
-- foreign country; and (b) the interests of justice require that the witness give the evidence." (5): if the witness
-- gives it, willingly or when required, "the coroner must give the witness a certificate". (6): "The coroner must also
-- give a witness a certificate under this section if-- (a) the objection has been overruled; and (b) after the evidence
-- has been given, the coroner finds that there were reasonable grounds for the objection." (11): "A certificate under
-- this section may only be given to a witness who is an individual."
DECLARE `Answer the question answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner may require me to answer (s 48(2)); if the answer may incriminate me or expose me to a civil penalty I can object (s 51B)`
    `yes: the coroner has overruled my objection; if the coroner later finds there were reasonable grounds for it, I must be given a certificate (s 51B(6))`
    `yes: the coroner may require me to answer, being satisfied it would not tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty and that the interests of justice require it, and must then give me a certificate (s 51B(4), (5))`
    `no: I need not give the evidence unless the coroner requires it under s 51B(4); if I give it willingly I must be given a certificate (s 51B(3), (5))`

@export Must I answer a coroner's question when the answer may incriminate me?
GIVEN `I object that the answer may incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you object to answering on the ground that the answer may tend to prove you committed an offence (in Australia or abroad) or are liable to a civil penalty
      `the coroner finds reasonable grounds for my objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner has decided there are reasonable grounds for your objection
      `the coroner is satisfied justice requires my answer and it shows no foreign offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner is satisfied both that the evidence would not tend to prove an offence or civil penalty under a foreign law and that the interests of justice require you to give it
GIVETH AN `Answer the question answer`
DECIDE `Must I answer this question question` IS
    IF (NOT `I object that the answer may incriminate me`)
    THEN `yes: the coroner may require me to answer (s 48(2)); if the answer may incriminate me or expose me to a civil penalty I can object (s 51B)`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the coroner finds reasonable grounds for my objection`)
         THEN `yes: the coroner has overruled my objection; if the coroner later finds there were reasonable grounds for it, I must be given a certificate (s 51B(6))`
         ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied justice requires my answer and it shows no foreign offence`
              THEN `yes: the coroner may require me to answer, being satisfied it would not tend to prove a foreign offence or penalty and that the interests of justice require it, and must then give me a certificate (s 51B(4), (5))`
              ELSE `no: I need not give the evidence unless the coroner requires it under s 51B(4); if I give it willingly I must be given a certificate (s 51B(3), (5))`

DECLARE `Certificate answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I gave the evidence after the coroner found reasonable grounds for my objection, so the coroner must give me a certificate (s 51B(5))`
    `yes: my objection was overruled, but after I gave the evidence the coroner found there were reasonable grounds for it, so the coroner must give me a certificate (s 51B(6))`
    `no: my objection was overruled and the coroner has not found that there were reasonable grounds for it (s 51B(6))`
    `not yet: the certificate is given once I have given the evidence (s 51B(5), (6))`
    `no: a s 51B certificate follows only an objection on the ground of self-incrimination or a civil penalty (s 51B(1))`
    `no: a s 51B certificate may only be given to a witness who is an individual (s 51B(11))`

@export Will I get a certificate protecting the evidence I gave over my objection?
GIVEN `I am an individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an individual (a natural person), not a company or other body
      `I objected that the evidence may incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you objected on the ground that the evidence may tend to prove you committed an offence or are liable to a civil penalty
      `the coroner found reasonable grounds for my objection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner decided, when you objected, that there were reasonable grounds for it
      `I have given the evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have now given the evidence (willingly or because the coroner required it)
      `the coroner later found my objection had reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your objection was overruled: whether, after you gave the evidence, the coroner found there were reasonable grounds for it
GIVETH A `Certificate answer`
DECIDE `Will I get a certificate question` IS
    IF (NOT `I am an individual`)
    THEN `no: a s 51B certificate may only be given to a witness who is an individual (s 51B(11))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `I objected that the evidence may incriminate me`)
         THEN `no: a s 51B certificate follows only an objection on the ground of self-incrimination or a civil penalty (s 51B(1))`
         ELSE IF (NOT `I have given the evidence`)
              THEN `not yet: the certificate is given once I have given the evidence (s 51B(5), (6))`
              ELSE IF `the coroner found reasonable grounds for my objection`
                   THEN `yes: I gave the evidence after the coroner found reasonable grounds for my objection, so the coroner must give me a certificate (s 51B(5))`
                   ELSE IF `the coroner later found my objection had reasonable grounds`
                        THEN `yes: my objection was overruled, but after I gave the evidence the coroner found there were reasonable grounds for it, so the coroner must give me a certificate (s 51B(6))`
                        ELSE `no: my objection was overruled and the coroner has not found that there were reasonable grounds for it (s 51B(6))`

-- s 51B(7): "In any proceeding in an ACT court or before any entity authorised by a territory law, or by consent of
-- parties, to hear, receive and examine evidence, the following evidence cannot be used against a person: (a) evidence
-- given by the person in relation to which a certificate under this section has been given; (b) evidence of any
-- information, document or thing obtained as a direct or indirect consequence of the person having given evidence."
-- (8): "However, subsection (7) does not apply to a criminal proceeding in relation to the falsity of the evidence."
-- (9): it has effect despite any challenge to the certificate.
DECLARE `Where it might be used` IS ONE OF
    `a proceeding in an ACT court, or before an entity authorised by a territory law or by consent to take evidence`
    `a criminal proceeding about whether my evidence was false`
    `a proceeding outside the ACT (in another State or Territory, a federal court, or overseas)`

DECLARE `Certified evidence answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: neither the evidence nor anything obtained because I gave it can be used against me there, even if the certificate is later challenged (s 51B(7), (9))`
    `yes: the protection does not apply to a criminal proceeding about the falsity of the evidence (s 51B(8))`
    `not covered: s 51B(7) speaks only of proceedings in an ACT court or before an entity authorised by a territory law or by consent; whether other laws protect me elsewhere is outside this Act`
    `s 51B gives me no protection for evidence without a certificate (other protections, such as s 43(4) for produced documents, may apply)`

@export Can evidence I gave at the inquest be used against me in another case?
GIVEN `I have a certificate for that evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner gave you a section 51B certificate for that evidence
      `where it might be used` IS A `Where it might be used` @desc Where it might be used against you. One of: a proceeding in an ACT court or ACT body that takes evidence; a criminal proceeding about whether your evidence was false; a proceeding outside the ACT
GIVETH A `Certified evidence answer`
DECIDE `Can my evidence be used against me question` IS
    IF (NOT `I have a certificate for that evidence`)
    THEN `s 51B gives me no protection for evidence without a certificate (other protections, such as s 43(4) for produced documents, may apply)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `where it might be used`
         WHEN `a proceeding in an ACT court, or before an entity authorised by a territory law or by consent to take evidence` THEN `no: neither the evidence nor anything obtained because I gave it can be used against me there, even if the certificate is later challenged (s 51B(7), (9))`
         WHEN `a criminal proceeding about whether my evidence was false` THEN `yes: the protection does not apply to a criminal proceeding about the falsity of the evidence (s 51B(8))`
         WHEN `a proceeding outside the ACT (in another State or Territory, a federal court, or overseas)` THEN `not covered: s 51B(7) speaks only of proceedings in an ACT court or before an entity authorised by a territory law or by consent; whether other laws protect me elsewhere is outside this Act`

§§ `s 55 -- adverse comment in the findings (only if the coroner proposes to criticise me)`

-- s 55(1): "A coroner must not include in a finding or report under this Act (including an annual report) a comment
-- adverse to a person identifiable from the finding or report unless the coroner has, making the finding or report, taken
-- all reasonable steps to give to the person a copy of the proposed comment and a written notice advising the person
-- that, within a specified period (being not more than 28 days and not less than 14 days after the date of the notice),
-- the person may-- (a) make a submission to the coroner in relation to the proposed comment; or (b) give to the coroner a
-- written statement in relation to it." (2): "The coroner may extend, by not more than 28 days, the period of time
-- specified in a notice under subsection (1)." (3): "If the person so requests, the coroner must include in the report
-- the statement given under subsection (1) (b) or a fair summary of it."
DECLARE `Adverse comment answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, but only after the notice period: the coroner has taken all reasonable steps to give me the proposed comment and a notice that I may make a submission or give a written statement within the period (s 55(1))`
    `no: the coroner must not include it unless the coroner has taken all reasonable steps to give me a copy of the proposed comment and written notice of my right to respond within 14 to 28 days (s 55(1))`
    `s 55 does not apply: it protects only a person identifiable from the finding or report against a comment adverse to them`

@export Can the coroner criticise me in the findings or report?
GIVEN `the comment is adverse to me and I can be identified` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proposed comment is adverse to you and you are identifiable from the finding or report
      `the coroner took all reasonable steps to give me the comment and notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the coroner took all reasonable steps to give you a copy of the proposed comment and a written notice of your right to make a submission or give a written statement within the stated period
GIVETH AN `Adverse comment answer`
DECIDE `Can the coroner criticise me question` IS
    IF (NOT `the comment is adverse to me and I can be identified`)
    THEN `s 55 does not apply: it protects only a person identifiable from the finding or report against a comment adverse to them`
    ELSE IF `the coroner took all reasonable steps to give me the comment and notice`
         THEN `yes, but only after the notice period: the coroner has taken all reasonable steps to give me the proposed comment and a notice that I may make a submission or give a written statement within the period (s 55(1))`
         ELSE `no: the coroner must not include it unless the coroner has taken all reasonable steps to give me a copy of the proposed comment and written notice of my right to respond within 14 to 28 days (s 55(1))`

DECLARE `Notice period answer` IS ONE OF
    `the period is within the limits: it ends between 14 and 28 days after the date of the notice (s 55(1))`
    `the period is too short: it must end at least 14 days after the date of the notice (s 55(1))`
    `the period is too long: it must end no more than 28 days after the date of the notice (s 55(1))`

@export Does the period the coroner gave me to respond to a proposed adverse comment meet the Act's limits?
GIVEN `the date of the notice` IS A DATE @desc The date of the coroner's written notice (ISO 8601)
      `the last day of the period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period the notice gives you to respond (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Notice period answer`
DECIDE `Is my response period lawful question` IS
    IF (NOT ((14 `days after` `the date of the notice`) `is on or before` `the last day of the period`))
    THEN `the period is too short: it must end at least 14 days after the date of the notice (s 55(1))`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`the last day of the period` `is on or before` (28 `days after` `the date of the notice`)))
         THEN `the period is too long: it must end no more than 28 days after the date of the notice (s 55(1))`
         ELSE `the period is within the limits: it ends between 14 and 28 days after the date of the notice (s 55(1))`

DECLARE `Response deadline answer` IS ONE OF
    `I may respond up to and including` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `the coroner cannot extend the period by more than 28 days (s 55(2)); at the most I may respond up to and including` HAS `the latest day` IS A DATE

@export By what day must I respond to the coroner's proposed adverse comment?
GIVEN `the last day of the period` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period stated in the coroner's notice (ISO 8601)
      `the extension in days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days the coroner has extended the period by (0 if none)
GIVETH A `Response deadline answer`
DECIDE `Last day to respond to an adverse comment question` IS
    IF `the extension in days` AT MOST 28
    THEN `I may respond up to and including` (`the extension in days` `days after` `the last day of the period`)
    ELSE `the coroner cannot extend the period by more than 28 days (s 55(2)); at the most I may respond up to and including` (28 `days after` `the last day of the period`)

DECLARE `My statement answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I asked, so the coroner must include my written statement, or a fair summary of it, in the report (s 55(3))`
    `only if I ask: the coroner must include my written statement, or a fair summary of it, in the report if I request it (s 55(3))`
    `not covered: s 55(3) speaks only of a report; it does not require the statement to be included in a finding`
    `no: s 55(3) concerns a written statement I gave under s 55(1)(b); a submission is not covered`

@export Must the coroner include my written response to an adverse comment?
GIVEN `I gave a written statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the coroner a written statement about the proposed comment (rather than only making a submission)
      `I asked for it to be included` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the coroner to include your statement
      `the comment is in a report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adverse comment is in a report (to the Attorney-General, or an annual report) rather than in the findings
GIVETH A `My statement answer`
DECIDE `Must my statement be included question` IS
    IF (NOT `I gave a written statement`)
    THEN `no: s 55(3) concerns a written statement I gave under s 55(1)(b); a submission is not covered`
    ELSE IF (NOT `the comment is in a report`)
         THEN `not covered: s 55(3) speaks only of a report; it does not require the statement to be included in a finding`
         ELSE IF `I asked for it to be included`
              THEN `yes: I asked, so the coroner must include my written statement, or a fair summary of it, in the report (s 55(3))`
              ELSE `only if I ask: the coroner must include my written statement, or a fair summary of it, in the report if I request it (s 55(3))`

§§ `s 25 -- the treating doctor at the post-mortem (only if I treated the person who died)`

-- s 25: "A doctor who attended a person professionally at or immediately before the person's death or during the person's
-- last illness is entitled, on request, to be present as an observer at a post-mortem examination of the body of the
-- deceased."
DECLARE `Observe the post-mortem answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as the doctor who attended the person at or immediately before death or in the last illness, I am entitled to be present as an observer if I ask (s 25)`
    `yes, if I ask: as the doctor who attended the person at or immediately before death or in the last illness, I am entitled to be present as an observer on request (s 25)`
    `not covered: s 25 entitles only a doctor who attended the person professionally at or immediately before the death or during the last illness`

@export I was the deceased's treating doctor. Can I attend the post-mortem examination?
GIVEN `I am a doctor who attended them at or just before death or in their last illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a doctor who attended the person professionally at or immediately before their death, or during their last illness
      `I have asked to be present` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked to be present at the post-mortem examination
GIVETH AN `Observe the post-mortem answer`
DECIDE `Can I observe the post-mortem as the treating doctor question` IS
    IF (NOT `I am a doctor who attended them at or just before death or in their last illness`)
    THEN `not covered: s 25 entitles only a doctor who attended the person professionally at or immediately before the death or during the last illness`
    ELSE IF `I have asked to be present`
         THEN `yes: as the doctor who attended the person at or immediately before death or in the last illness, I am entitled to be present as an observer if I ask (s 25)`
         ELSE `yes, if I ask: as the doctor who attended the person at or immediately before death or in the last illness, I am entitled to be present as an observer on request (s 25)`

§§ `ss 98, 99B -- expenses, and protection for telling the coroner`

-- s 98: "A coroner may allow a witness who gives evidence before the coroner, whether or not the witness was subpoenaed to
-- attend, witness expenses assessed in accordance with the Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 4." s 44(2)(b): a
-- subpoena must come with "a form to be completed by the person to claim the person's reasonable costs and expenses of
-- attendance". s 46(2): the same for an order to give evidence before the hearing. The schedule 4 amounts are not
-- deposited, so no amount is computed.
DECLARE `Expenses answer` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the coroner may allow me witness expenses, assessed under the Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 4, whether or not I was subpoenaed (s 98); it is a discretion`
    `no: s 98 covers a witness who gives evidence before the coroner`

@export Can I claim my expenses for giving evidence at the inquest?
GIVEN `I gave evidence before the coroner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave evidence before the coroner (with or without a subpoena)
GIVETH AN `Expenses answer`
DECIDE `Can I claim witness expenses question` IS
    IF `I gave evidence before the coroner`
    THEN `possibly: the coroner may allow me witness expenses, assessed under the Court Procedures Rules 2006, schedule 4, whether or not I was subpoenaed (s 98); it is a discretion`
    ELSE `no: s 98 covers a witness who gives evidence before the coroner`

-- s 99B(1): "An entity may give information to a coroner if-- (a) a coroner asks the entity for the information in
-- connection with the exercise of the coroner's functions under this Act; or (b) the entity believes on reasonable grounds
-- that the information is relevant to the exercise of the coroner's functions under this Act." (2): "If an entity gives
-- information to a coroner under this section-- (a) giving the information is not-- (i) a breach of confidence; or (ii) a
-- breach of professional etiquette or ethics; or (iii) a breach of a rule of professional conduct; and (b) the entity does
-- not incur civil or criminal liability only because of giving the information; and (c) for an entity who is a public
-- servant--the entity is not liable to administrative action (including disciplinary action or dismissal) only because of
-- giving the information." Example: a theatre nurse present at an operation where the patient died unexpectedly.
DECLARE `Protection answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: giving the information is not a breach of confidence, professional ethics or a rule of professional conduct, and I incur no civil or criminal liability only because I gave it (s 99B(2)(a), (b))`
    `yes: giving the information is not a breach of confidence, professional ethics or a rule of professional conduct; I incur no civil or criminal liability, and as a public servant face no disciplinary action or dismissal, only because I gave it (s 99B(2))`
    `no: s 99B protects information given only when the coroner asked for it or I believe on reasonable grounds that it is relevant to the coroner's functions`

@export Am I protected if I give the coroner information, for example confidential patient or workplace information?
GIVEN `the coroner asked me for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner asked you for the information in connection with the coroner's functions
      `I believe on reasonable grounds it is relevant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you believe on reasonable grounds that the information is relevant to the coroner's functions
      `I am a public servant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a public servant
GIVETH A `Protection answer`
DECIDE `Am I protected for telling the coroner question` IS
    IF `the coroner asked me for it` OR `I believe on reasonable grounds it is relevant`
    THEN IF `I am a public servant`
         THEN `yes: giving the information is not a breach of confidence, professional ethics or a rule of professional conduct; I incur no civil or criminal liability, and as a public servant face no disciplinary action or dismissal, only because I gave it (s 99B(2))`
         ELSE `yes: giving the information is not a breach of confidence, professional ethics or a rule of professional conduct, and I incur no civil or criminal liability only because I gave it (s 99B(2)(a), (b))`
    ELSE `no: s 99B protects information given only when the coroner asked for it or I believe on reasonable grounds that it is relevant to the coroner's functions`

§§ `ss 19B, 100 -- the deceased's records (only if I hold them)`

-- s 19B(2): "A coroner may, by order, direct a person who has responsibility for or control of any medical records
-- relating to the deceased person, including someone in charge of a hospital or residential institution, to give the
-- records to the coroner or the doctor stated in the order." (4): the order must be in writing. (3): a doctor given the
-- records must return them as soon as reasonably practicable after the post-mortem is dispensed with or completed.
DECLARE `Medical records answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner has directed me by written order to give the records to the coroner or the doctor named in it (s 19B(2), (4)); a doctor given them must return them once the post-mortem is dispensed with or completed (s 19B(3))`
    `not unless the coroner orders it in writing: the coroner may direct the person responsible for or in control of the records to give them (s 19B(2), (4)); I may still give them voluntarily (s 99B)`
    `no: a s 19B order is directed to a person who has responsibility for or control of the deceased's medical records`

@export Must I hand over the deceased person's medical records to the coroner?
GIVEN `I am responsible for or control the records` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have responsibility for or control of medical records about the deceased (for example as a doctor, or the person in charge of a hospital, aged care or disability facility)
      `the coroner has ordered me in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a coroner has given you a written order to give the records to the coroner or a named doctor
GIVETH A `Medical records answer`
DECIDE `Must I hand over the medical records question` IS
    IF (NOT `I am responsible for or control the records`)
    THEN `no: a s 19B order is directed to a person who has responsibility for or control of the deceased's medical records`
    ELSE IF `the coroner has ordered me in writing`
         THEN `yes: the coroner has directed me by written order to give the records to the coroner or the doctor named in it (s 19B(2), (4)); a doctor given them must return them once the post-mortem is dispensed with or completed (s 19B(3))`
         ELSE `not unless the coroner orders it in writing: the coroner may direct the person responsible for or in control of the records to give them (s 19B(2), (4)); I may still give them voluntarily (s 99B)`

-- s 100(1): "If a person dies while a patient in a hospital or other institution in circumstances in which a coroner has
-- jurisdiction to hold an inquest, the person in charge of the hospital or institution must ensure that all records
-- relating to the person who died are kept for at least 3 years after the day of the death. Maximum penalty: 50 penalty
-- units." (2): "If a person dies in care or custody, the responsible person must ensure that all records relating to the
-- person who died are kept for at least 7 years after the day of the death. Maximum penalty: 50 penalty units." (3):
-- strict liability. (4): the responsible person is the person in charge of the hospital or institution, or otherwise of
-- the agency the deceased was in the care or custody of.
-- READING: "kept for at least 3 years after the day of the death" is kept to the end of the day 3 years on.
DECLARE `Records answer` IS ONE OF
    `I must keep all the records up to and including` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `s 100 does not apply: the person did not die in care or custody, nor as a patient of my hospital or institution in circumstances giving a coroner jurisdiction`

@export I am in charge of the hospital, institution or agency where a person died. Until what day must I keep their records?
GIVEN `the day of the death` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died (ISO 8601)
      `they died in care or custody` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was a death in care or a death in custody, and you are in charge of the hospital, institution or agency responsible
      `they died as my patient in circumstances giving a coroner jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they died while a patient in the hospital or institution you are in charge of, in circumstances in which a coroner has jurisdiction to hold an inquest
GIVETH A `Records answer`
DECIDE `How long must I keep the records question` IS
    IF `they died in care or custody`
    THEN `I must keep all the records up to and including` (7 `years after` `the day of the death`)
    ELSE IF `they died as my patient in circumstances giving a coroner jurisdiction`
         THEN `I must keep all the records up to and including` (3 `years after` `the day of the death`)
         ELSE `s 100 does not apply: the person did not die in care or custody, nor as a patient of my hospital or institution in circumstances giving a coroner jurisdiction`

-- The s 100 duty as a regulative rule: the records must not be disposed of within the period, counted in days from the
-- day of the death. A disposal in the period is a breach (strict liability, maximum 50 penalty units).
DECLARE `Records party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the person in charge`

DECLARE `Records act` IS ONE OF
    `dispose of the records of the person who died`

GIVEN `the days to keep them` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Records party` `Records act`
DECIDE `s 100 -- I must keep the records` `the days to keep them` IS
    PARTY `I, the person in charge`
    SHANT `dispose of the records of the person who died`
    WITHIN `the days to keep them`
    LEST  BREACH BY `I, the person in charge` BECAUSE "s 100: the records of the person who died were not kept for the period s 100 requires (an offence of strict liability, maximum 50 penalty units)"

-- The number of days in the period, from the day of the death.
GIVEN `the day of the death` IS A DATE, `years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days in the keeping period` `the day of the death` `years` MEANS
    DATE_SERIAL (`years` `years after` `the day of the death`) MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day of the death`
